1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:10,200
They lie in silence, cities within cities, where a tomb is a home, where a fire isn't cheery,

2
00:00:10,201 --> 00:00:17,440
where your name is sandblasted into stone, and your transportation is one way.

3
00:00:18,320 --> 00:00:24,140
Let us be your guide to a land you will someday enter, and never leave.

4
00:00:24,740 --> 00:00:28,660
Now, Cemeteries on Modern Marvels.

5
00:00:50,960 --> 00:00:54,960
Since the first man walked the Earth, more than 100 billion

6
00:00:54,961 --> 00:00:58,960
people have been born.

7
00:00:58,961 --> 00:01:03,200
And although we mortal souls live longer than ever before,

8
00:01:03,201 --> 00:01:08,720
inevitably, we wind up in places like these, with only ghosts and

9
00:01:08,721 --> 00:01:15,720
worms for friends. No fewer than 2 million people die

10
00:01:15,721 --> 00:01:23,720
in the United States each year. And that makes dealing with our

11
00:01:23,721 --> 00:01:30,720
our dead, a $20 billion industry.

12
00:01:30,721 --> 00:01:37,060
America witnesses approximately 5,500 burials a day.

13
00:01:37,061 --> 00:01:40,060
Most of us will kick back in caskets.

14
00:01:40,061 --> 00:01:48,980
About 25% of the dead will have their corporeal form incinerated.

15
00:01:48,981 --> 00:01:52,820
Others take a more exotic journey into the next world,

16
00:01:52,821 --> 00:01:57,820
including launching one's remains into space.

17
00:01:57,920 --> 00:01:59,860
But most corpses take up residence

18
00:01:59,960 --> 00:02:05,460
in those cities of the dead we call cemeteries.

19
00:02:05,560 --> 00:02:07,860
Well, they're called cities of the dead.

20
00:02:07,960 --> 00:02:10,900
For the most immediate reason is that they resemble small scale

21
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:15,940
cities, particularly if you look at an aerial view of them.

22
00:02:16,040 --> 00:02:17,540
We plan them carefully.

23
00:02:17,640 --> 00:02:20,640
We have business in them.

24
00:02:20,641 --> 00:02:24,440
We have deviance that occurs in them.

25
00:02:24,540 --> 00:02:29,440
They, in some sense, are both these sacred realms

26
00:02:29,540 --> 00:02:32,680
that are outside of our daily routine lives.

27
00:02:32,780 --> 00:02:36,580
And at the same time, they are reflections of those

28
00:02:36,680 --> 00:02:39,500
and part of those and, in some sense, shape those.

29
00:02:42,300 --> 00:02:45,500
Above ground, the human history of cemeteries

30
00:02:45,600 --> 00:02:48,000
is made of stone and sings of eternity.

31
00:02:50,640 --> 00:02:55,340
But beneath, that history is made of flesh forever altered

32
00:02:55,341 --> 00:02:58,580
by the insidious process of decomposition.

33
00:03:02,680 --> 00:03:06,800
In as little as 10 years, the skin of the corpse begins to

34
00:03:06,801 --> 00:03:12,800
loosen and sag, turning to liquid and toxic gas, or in

35
00:03:12,801 --> 00:03:18,400
extreme conditions, drying to a mummified mask.

36
00:03:18,401 --> 00:03:22,900
The bones, even in a well-made casket, remain whole only for a

37
00:03:22,901 --> 00:03:23,900
few hundred years.

38
00:03:27,700 --> 00:03:32,940
Nonetheless, it's comforting to think of death as peaceful slumber

39
00:03:32,941 --> 00:03:40,660
and cemeteries as pleasant places, where a single soul may

40
00:03:40,661 --> 00:03:44,560
be glorified, or millions speak in a single voice.

41
00:03:44,561 --> 00:03:51,560
At the dawn of history, the dead were left to rot or were devoured

42
00:03:51,561 --> 00:03:52,560
by vultures.

43
00:03:54,560 --> 00:03:58,560
Later, traditions developed in which heavy stones were placed

44
00:03:58,561 --> 00:04:03,560
on the corpse, not only by way of marking the grave, but also to

45
00:04:03,561 --> 00:04:07,560
prevent the dead from rising, to walk among the living.

46
00:04:09,560 --> 00:04:13,560
Superstitious people feared that the dead might not stay in the

47
00:04:13,561 --> 00:04:14,900
might not stay that way.

48
00:04:18,800 --> 00:04:21,460
Someone who was ill would be put in a cave,

49
00:04:21,461 --> 00:04:24,040
and rocks would be put in front of the cave,

50
00:04:24,041 --> 00:04:26,340
so that if they got well, they could move the rocks.

51
00:04:26,341 --> 00:04:30,440
But in some cases, they didn't, and they became early tombs.

52
00:04:30,441 --> 00:04:35,680
As early as 50,000 BC, Neanderthals dug graves

53
00:04:35,681 --> 00:04:39,860
and scattered broken bones as offerings.

54
00:04:39,861 --> 00:04:42,520
There is evidence they marked their burial places

55
00:04:42,521 --> 00:04:48,520
flowers. In 3200 BC, the Egyptians developed

56
00:04:48,521 --> 00:04:52,520
the art of embalming to ensure their royals could contact the

57
00:04:52,521 --> 00:04:56,520
beyond. According to the historian

58
00:04:56,521 --> 00:05:01,520
Herodotus, the Egyptian embalming method required 80 days.

59
00:05:01,521 --> 00:05:06,520
First, the corpse's brain was pulled out through his nose.

60
00:05:06,521 --> 00:05:11,520
Then the corpse was soaked in brine for 30 days.

61
00:05:11,521 --> 00:05:15,760
After drying out, the desiccated body was placed in a wooden

62
00:05:15,820 --> 00:05:20,820
coffin, surrounded by a stone sarcophagus.

63
00:05:20,900 --> 00:05:24,760
For their trouble, Egyptian embalmers produced a rather

64
00:05:24,840 --> 00:05:29,340
off-putting result, blackened, dry skin stretched tightly over

65
00:05:29,400 --> 00:05:34,040
a chemically tanned body. But they believed such a body

66
00:05:34,100 --> 00:05:38,180
would attract the psychic double, the astral being who would lead

67
00:05:38,181 --> 00:05:43,480
who would lead the deceased to the everlasting.

68
00:05:43,580 --> 00:05:47,760
By 800 BC, cremation was the most common method of body

69
00:05:47,860 --> 00:05:51,860
disposal in Greece. The Greeks coined the word

70
00:05:51,960 --> 00:05:56,500
koimatyrion, or sleeping place, from which the word cemetery

71
00:05:56,600 --> 00:06:03,480
is derived. In 600 BC, the cremation

72
00:06:03,580 --> 00:06:06,980
tradition spread to the Roman Empire.

73
00:06:06,981 --> 00:06:11,460
One reason that the Romans embraced cremation

74
00:06:11,560 --> 00:06:14,920
was hygienic concerns, that the cities themselves

75
00:06:15,020 --> 00:06:17,620
were so lacking in hygiene that they

76
00:06:17,720 --> 00:06:21,220
feared the contamination that was associated

77
00:06:21,320 --> 00:06:24,740
with the decomposing body.

78
00:06:24,840 --> 00:06:28,540
In 400 AD, however, cremation's flames

79
00:06:28,640 --> 00:06:32,740
were dampened, Christianity was on the rise,

80
00:06:32,840 --> 00:06:36,980
and Emperor Constantine declared burning the dead to

81
00:06:36,981 --> 00:06:42,720
The alternative, earth burial, had deep significance for early

82
00:06:42,721 --> 00:06:44,720
Christians.

83
00:06:44,721 --> 00:06:49,220
Their churches were built over tombs of martyrs.

84
00:06:49,221 --> 00:06:54,220
In Rome, for example, Saint Peter is buried beneath an altar

85
00:06:54,221 --> 00:06:57,040
bearing his name.

86
00:07:00,940 --> 00:07:05,380
The rich and powerful arrange to be buried in consecrated ground

87
00:07:05,381 --> 00:07:09,380
inside the church. Christians believed that such a burial

88
00:07:09,420 --> 00:07:13,380
meant the body would rise again come Judgment Day.

89
00:07:16,420 --> 00:07:19,420
Those who couldn't afford a proper burial ended up in mass

90
00:07:19,460 --> 00:07:24,460
graves in potters' fields. The term came about because the

91
00:07:24,500 --> 00:07:30,380
first field so used may have belonged to a potter.

92
00:07:30,400 --> 00:07:35,380
To be buried in the church is to be as close to God, as close

93
00:07:35,381 --> 00:07:41,680
to that magical relationship between human and God as possible.

94
00:07:41,681 --> 00:07:44,120
Very quickly, of course, the church

95
00:07:44,121 --> 00:07:47,120
becomes overcrowded with burials,

96
00:07:47,121 --> 00:07:50,420
and it becomes impossible to satisfy all the claims

97
00:07:50,421 --> 00:07:51,220
on that space.

98
00:07:54,140 --> 00:07:58,980
In 1374, a dark cloud appeared over Europe,

99
00:07:58,981 --> 00:08:01,080
the Black Plague.

100
00:08:01,081 --> 00:08:04,680
Carried by rodents infected with a deadly virus,

101
00:08:04,681 --> 00:08:08,320
The plague brought death to millions within just 18 months.

102
00:08:11,520 --> 00:08:15,720
As cemeteries spilled over with bodies and bones,

103
00:08:15,721 --> 00:08:21,640
horrified eyewitnesses reported on the end stage of the disease.

104
00:08:21,641 --> 00:08:25,440
Those infected felt themselves penetrated by a pain

105
00:08:25,441 --> 00:08:27,140
throughout their whole bodies.

106
00:08:27,141 --> 00:08:30,540
Then vomiting of blood continued without intermission

107
00:08:30,541 --> 00:08:33,980
for three days, there being no means of healing it.

108
00:08:33,981 --> 00:08:36,980
and then the patient expired.

109
00:08:40,980 --> 00:08:45,120
As the plague spread, churches were clogged with the dead,

110
00:08:45,121 --> 00:08:48,620
who were stacked up like cordwood.

111
00:08:48,621 --> 00:08:52,240
There was no choice but to move cemeteries out of the church

112
00:08:52,241 --> 00:08:56,040
and into the churchyard. When space grew precious there

113
00:08:56,041 --> 00:09:02,180
in the 18th century, cemeteries moved beyond church property.

114
00:09:02,181 --> 00:09:04,820
The dead had little rest in Europe.

115
00:09:04,920 --> 00:09:09,320
Burial and crypt facilities there were rented, not owned.

116
00:09:09,420 --> 00:09:12,120
That meant last year's bodies were cleared out,

117
00:09:12,220 --> 00:09:17,760
ground to dust, and new terminal tenants took their place.

118
00:09:17,860 --> 00:09:20,500
In many European cemeteries, even today,

119
00:09:20,600 --> 00:09:24,500
you pay a fee for a specific period of time.

120
00:09:24,600 --> 00:09:26,540
That period of time can be 20 years.

121
00:09:26,640 --> 00:09:27,840
It can be 100 years.

122
00:09:27,841 --> 00:09:34,340
In the 1780s, authorities in Paris even built an underground

123
00:09:34,341 --> 00:09:41,280
boneyard called the Catacombs. But as the numbers of the dead

124
00:09:41,281 --> 00:09:46,340
grew, the city fathers had no choice but to give them more

125
00:09:46,341 --> 00:09:49,340
space. Père Lachaise Cemetery,

126
00:09:49,341 --> 00:09:53,840
established over 200 years ago, still offers permanent burial

127
00:09:53,841 --> 00:10:02,180
within its spacious walls. In frontier America, cemeteries

128
00:10:02,181 --> 00:10:07,080
were not yet as developed as their European counterparts.

129
00:10:07,081 --> 00:10:10,800
Even the grave markers were rough-hewn hand-lettered notices,

130
00:10:10,801 --> 00:10:17,300
speaking of violent ends, mass murders, and lynchings.

131
00:10:17,301 --> 00:10:20,900
Unlike in Europe, space in American cemeteries was never

132
00:10:20,901 --> 00:10:22,900
cemeteries was never rented.

133
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:25,900
You could decay at your own leisure.

134
00:10:28,900 --> 00:10:32,740
But as cemeteries inevitably filled up, the dead in America

135
00:10:32,840 --> 00:10:36,140
began to take up valuable real estate that the living felt

136
00:10:36,240 --> 00:10:39,060
