00:35:05,581 --> 00:35:12,580
very close contact with death, to one where you have open vistas.

498
00:35:12,581 --> 00:35:18,580
It's still a natural landscape, but it's a controlled, in aesthetic words, beautiful landscape.

499
00:35:19,580 --> 00:35:24,580
At Forest Lawn, computer-assisted designs deliver the appropriate

500
00:35:24,581 --> 00:35:30,580
crypts, graves, and tombs. Graves are dug in advance and a

501
00:35:30,581 --> 00:35:37,580
cement vault set in place. Then the hole is filled and

502
00:35:37,581 --> 00:35:42,580
covered with fresh green sod, ready for its future occupant.

503
00:35:42,581 --> 00:35:46,580
When we start with a master plan, we define the boundaries

504
00:35:46,581 --> 00:35:49,920
the boundaries and we define the types of properties that are

505
00:35:50,020 --> 00:35:53,280
needed in order to maintain our inventory, which is obviously

506
00:35:53,380 --> 00:35:59,820
driven on by demand. The vault weighs 700 pounds.

507
00:35:59,920 --> 00:36:03,400
Forest Lawn engineers custom designed a vault carrier to

508
00:36:03,500 --> 00:36:10,400
lower it into place. Then, as has been customary for

509
00:36:10,500 --> 00:36:15,780
centuries, human muscle, aided by a winch, is required to

510
00:36:15,781 --> 00:36:20,780
to lower the casket into the ground.

511
00:36:20,781 --> 00:36:23,780
Today, ground burial is the most popular method

512
00:36:23,781 --> 00:36:27,780
of body disposal. But an age-old alternative

513
00:36:27,781 --> 00:36:32,780
is making a comeback. Next, it's the hottest place

514
00:36:32,781 --> 00:36:39,780
in town, about 1,800 degrees, the crematory.

515
00:36:39,781 --> 00:36:44,780
Varanasi is India's city of the dead.

516
00:36:44,781 --> 00:36:49,180
Situated on the northern bank of the Ganges River, the city

517
00:36:49,280 --> 00:36:53,580
features a hundred ghats, or steps.

518
00:36:53,680 --> 00:36:57,720
Many are used for bathing in the sacred waters.

519
00:36:57,820 --> 00:37:04,040
But one of the oldest is for burning.

520
00:37:04,140 --> 00:37:08,840
Cremation on a pyre of burning wood is said to free the soul

521
00:37:08,940 --> 00:37:12,780
from the cycle of reincarnation.

522
00:37:12,781 --> 00:37:18,580
Each month, hundreds of corpses are disposed of here, literally

523
00:37:18,581 --> 00:37:22,780
requiring boatloads of wood to fuel fires.

524
00:37:26,780 --> 00:37:30,580
Cremation was also a tradition of the Vikings, who through the

525
00:37:30,581 --> 00:37:34,380
8th and 11th centuries set their most venerated dead to sea

526
00:37:34,381 --> 00:37:40,580
aboard burning ships. The Greek tradition of cremation

527
00:37:40,581 --> 00:37:46,580
called for placing the remains in box-shaped vessels.

528
00:37:46,680 --> 00:37:50,020
As cremation migrated from India to America,

529
00:37:50,120 --> 00:37:52,820
it traveled indoors for health reasons

530
00:37:52,920 --> 00:37:56,920
into sealed cremation chambers.

531
00:37:57,020 --> 00:38:02,540
In 1876, a retired physician named Francis Julius Lemoyne

532
00:38:02,640 --> 00:38:06,580
lit the first crematory in America.

533
00:38:06,680 --> 00:38:10,560
But actually, Dr. Lemoyne was not the first to go in his

534
00:38:10,580 --> 00:38:14,280
device. The body of a local baron was pressed

535
00:38:14,281 --> 00:38:20,620
into service by enthusiastic cremation advocates, who wanted to show a doubting public that cremation

536
00:38:20,621 --> 00:38:24,582
was a sanitary way to dispose of bodies.

537
00:38:24,583 --> 00:38:29,040
Faced with zinc, topped with corrugated iron,

538
00:38:29,500 --> 00:38:32,900
Dr. Lemoyne's crematory cost $1,500 to build.

539
00:38:34,640 --> 00:38:39,940
By 1900, 20 crematories were glowing in seven American cities.

540
00:38:40,580 --> 00:38:45,080
In 1913, there were 52 crematories in North America,

541
00:38:45,081 --> 00:38:48,580
and over 10,000 cremations took place in that year.

542
00:38:55,920 --> 00:39:00,580
Today, cremations are performed in computer-controlled chambers.

543
00:39:00,581 --> 00:39:11,580
The procedure takes place inside a casket-shaped container made of

544
00:39:11,581 --> 00:39:18,580
particle board that is rigid, combustible, and leak-proof.

545
00:39:18,581 --> 00:39:23,580
After the container is loaded in and the door sealed, a series of

546
00:39:23,581 --> 00:39:28,580
computer-controlled burners are activated.

547
00:39:28,581 --> 00:39:33,680
The heat inside the chamber must reach as high as 1,800 degrees

548
00:39:33,780 --> 00:39:37,520
to consume a corpse over a period of 1 and 1 half hours.

549
00:39:40,620 --> 00:39:43,420
The main burner aims a column of flame directly

550
00:39:43,520 --> 00:39:44,820
at the chest cavity.

551
00:39:44,920 --> 00:39:47,500
This part of the body contains the most fat

552
00:39:47,600 --> 00:39:50,300
and burns most efficiently.

553
00:39:50,400 --> 00:39:53,440
Later in the process, an afterburner kicks in

554
00:39:53,540 --> 00:39:56,740
to ensure that any particulate matter is burned completely

555
00:39:56,741 --> 00:39:59,740
before it is expelled into the air.

556
00:39:59,741 --> 00:40:01,740
When I started in the cremation profession,

557
00:40:01,741 --> 00:40:03,740
a crematory had four buttons,

558
00:40:03,741 --> 00:40:05,740
stop, start, open, and close.

559
00:40:05,741 --> 00:40:08,740
You can see today that the crematories have come a long way.

560
00:40:08,741 --> 00:40:11,740
The dials and the gadgets you see today

561
00:40:11,741 --> 00:40:12,740
are electronically controlled.

562
00:40:12,741 --> 00:40:14,740
They're computer generated.

563
00:40:14,741 --> 00:40:18,740
You have different gauges that will be able to start the crematory,

564
00:40:18,741 --> 00:40:21,740
determine the amount of time the cremation process will take place,

565
00:40:21,741 --> 00:40:25,740
the amount of air that will be used during the combustion process,

566
00:40:25,741 --> 00:40:29,180
the air mixed with the gas, and be able to determine if

567
00:40:29,200 --> 00:40:33,280
particulates are going into the stack, there's a gauge that'll be

568
00:40:33,320 --> 00:40:37,680
able to adjust the amount of burn to eliminate any emissions

569
00:40:37,720 --> 00:40:40,680
through the crematory itself.

570
00:40:40,720 --> 00:40:43,620
Families who want to see their loved ones off can watch the

571
00:40:43,660 --> 00:40:46,560
cremation from an observation room.

572
00:40:46,600 --> 00:40:49,660
With the multicultural changes in the United States, with

573
00:40:49,700 --> 00:40:53,940
religions wanting to be able to be part of the cremation process,

574
00:40:53,941 --> 00:40:57,780
members wanting to be part of the closure, a room like this,

575
00:40:57,880 --> 00:41:01,380
crematories built such as this are becoming more popular.

576
00:41:04,680 --> 00:41:08,380
Indians often have their first born son push the button,

577
00:41:08,480 --> 00:41:11,900
echoing the tradition of the funeral pyre in India.

578
00:41:17,200 --> 00:41:20,400
The end result of cremation is not ashes, as is commonly

579
00:41:20,401 --> 00:41:24,800
as is commonly believed, but bone fragments.

580
00:41:24,801 --> 00:41:26,600
Depending upon the size of the body,

581
00:41:26,601 --> 00:41:29,000
from three to nine pounds of these fragments

582
00:41:29,001 --> 00:41:31,800
remain to be placed in the cremation urn, or box.

583
00:41:34,840 --> 00:41:38,380
Of course, once the cremation remains are placed in an urn,

584
00:41:38,381 --> 00:41:42,160
they may be cast to sea, or even sent aloft.

585
00:41:47,440 --> 00:41:49,540
A company called Celestis offers

586
00:41:49,541 --> 00:41:52,540
to shoot about 8 ounces of one's remains into space.

587
00:41:55,620 --> 00:41:59,080
Psychologist and LSD advocate Timothy Leary

588
00:41:59,081 --> 00:42:01,900
and Star Trek creator Gene Roddenberry

589
00:42:01,901 --> 00:42:04,180
have taken that long, strange trip.

590
00:42:07,240 --> 00:42:11,500
The cost is $5,300, about the price of the average funeral.

591
00:42:14,380 --> 00:42:19,000
Placing the ashes in lunar orbit costs more, about $13,000.

592
00:42:19,540 --> 00:42:24,540
Cemeteries have changed throughout history to reflect

593
00:42:24,640 --> 00:42:28,540
